module.exports = {
  title: 'Routing with Vue Router',
  slug: 'routing',
  order: 6,
  topics: [
    {
      title: 'Router Setup, Route Params and Navigation',
      slug: 'router-setup-params',
      order: 0,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Routing is the backbone of any non-trivial Vue application: it maps the URL to the component tree, keeps the back button meaningful, and makes every screen deep-linkable. Interviewers probe it because the failure modes are so revealing. "Why doesn't my component update when the id in the URL changes?" and "why does a hard refresh 404?" are the two questions that separate people who have shipped a routed SPA from people who have followed a tutorial.</p>`,
        `<p>Both have precise answers — component reuse and server configuration respectively — and both cause production bugs that are hard to diagnose if you do not know the mechanism.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>Vue Router maps <strong>URL patterns to components</strong>. You register an ordered array of routes; the router matches the current URL against them and renders the winner into a <code>&lt;RouterView&gt;</code>.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">const routes = [\n  { path: '/', component: Home },\n  { path: '/users/:id', component: UserDetail },   // :id is a route param\n  { path: '/:pathMatch(.*)*', component: NotFound }, // catch-all, must be LAST\n]</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Two kinds of dynamic data live in a URL, and mixing them up is a classic mistake:</p>`,
        `<table>`,
        `<thead><tr><th></th><th>Route param</th><th>Query param</th></tr></thead>`,
        `<tbody>`,
        `<tr><td>Looks like</td><td><code>/users/42</code></td><td><code>/users?sort=name&amp;page=2</code></td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Declared in the route?</td><td>Yes — <code>path: '/users/:id'</code></td><td>No — freeform</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Required?</td><td>Part of the match</td><td>Always optional</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Order matters?</td><td>Yes, positional</td><td>No</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Read via</td><td><code>route.params.id</code></td><td><code>route.query.sort</code></td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Use for</td><td><em>Which</em> resource</td><td><em>How</em> to view it — filters, sort, pagination</td></tr>`,
        `</tbody>`,
        `</table>`,
        `<p>The rule of thumb: if removing it should 404, it is a route param; if removing it should just show defaults, it is a query param.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch7-url-anatomy.svg|Anatomy of a URL showing the path with a route param, the query string, and the hash, mapped to route.params, route.query and route.hash|Route params identify the resource and are part of the match; query params are optional modifiers of the same view.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>History modes.</strong> <code>createWebHistory()</code> uses the HTML5 History API and gives clean URLs — and requires server configuration, because the server will be asked for <code>/users/42</code> and must return <code>index.html</code> instead of 404ing. <code>createWebHashHistory()</code> puts everything after a <code>#</code>, which the server never sees, so it works on any static host with no configuration — at the cost of ugly URLs and worse SEO. <code>createMemoryHistory()</code> keeps history in memory and is for SSR and tests.</p>`,
        `<p>That first point is the "blank page on refresh" bug in full: in-app navigation is handled entirely by the client router, but a hard refresh sends the whole path to the server. Without a rewrite rule, the server has no idea what <code>/users/42</code> means.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Matching</strong> is top-down and first-win, so order is priority. The catch-all <code>/:pathMatch(.*)*</code> must be last or it shadows everything below it. Params support modifiers: <code>:id?</code> optional, <code>:id+</code> one or more, <code>:id*</code> zero or more, and custom regex like <code>:id(\\d+)</code> to match only digits.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Reading params.</strong> <code>useRoute()</code> returns a reactive route object; <code>useRouter()</code> returns the router instance for programmatic navigation. Every param value is a <strong>string</strong> — URLs have no type system — so <code>route.params.id === 42</code> is always false and conversion is your job.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>The component-reuse trap</strong> is the most important mechanic here. Navigating from <code>/users/1</code> to <code>/users/2</code> matches the <em>same route record</em>, so Vue Router reuses the component instance: no unmount, no remount, no <code>onMounted</code>. Anything you did in <code>onMounted</code> — such as fetching the user — never re-runs, and the page silently shows the previous user's data. Three fixes, in order of preference: watch the param, use <code>props: true</code> and watch the prop, or force a remount with <code>:key="route.fullPath"</code>.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>props: true</code></strong> decouples the component from the router entirely: params arrive as props, so the component is a plain input-driven component that can be unit-tested without a router and reused outside a route.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// router/index.js\nimport { createRouter, createWebHistory } from 'vue-router'\n\nconst routes = [\n  { path: '/', name: 'home', component: () =&gt; import('@/views/HomeView.vue') },\n\n  {\n    path: '/users/:id',\n    name: 'user-detail',\n    component: () =&gt; import('@/views/UserDetail.vue'),\n    props: true,                       // params → props\n  },\n\n  // regex constraint: only digits match, so /users/abc falls through\n  { path: '/orders/:id(\\\\d+)', component: OrderDetail },\n\n  // optional param\n  { path: '/search/:term?', component: SearchView },\n\n  // static props, or a function for full control\n  {\n    path: '/products/:id',\n    component: ProductView,\n    props: (route) =&gt; ({ id: Number(route.params.id), tab: route.query.tab ?? 'info' }),\n  },\n\n  { path: '/old-path', redirect: '/new-path' },\n  { path: '/legacy/:id', redirect: (to) =&gt; \`/users/\${to.params.id}\` },\n\n  // catch-all — MUST be last\n  { path: '/:pathMatch(.*)*', name: 'not-found', component: NotFound },\n]\n\nexport default createRouter({\n  history: createWebHistory(import.meta.env.BASE_URL),\n  routes,\n  // restore scroll on back/forward, honour #anchors\n  scrollBehavior(to, from, savedPosition) {\n    if (savedPosition) return savedPosition\n    if (to.hash) return { el: to.hash, behavior: 'smooth' }\n    return { top: 0 }\n  },\n})\n\n// main.js:  app.use(router)  ← BEFORE mount()</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The reuse trap and its fixes:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- ❌ Broken: navigating /users/1 → /users/2 reuses the instance,\n     so onMounted never re-runs and the old user stays on screen. --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref, onMounted } from 'vue'\nimport { useRoute } from 'vue-router'\n\nconst route = useRoute()\nconst user = ref(null)\nonMounted(async () =&gt; { user.value = await api.getUser(route.params.id) })\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- ✅ Fix 1: watch the param (immediate replaces onMounted) --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref, watch } from 'vue'\nimport { useRoute } from 'vue-router'\n\nconst route = useRoute()\nconst user = ref(null)\n\nwatch(\n  () =&gt; route.params.id,\n  async (id) =&gt; { user.value = await api.getUser(id) },\n  { immediate: true },\n)\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- ✅ Fix 2 (preferred): props: true — the component never touches the router --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nconst props = defineProps({ id: { type: String, required: true } })\nconst user = ref(null)\nwatch(() =&gt; props.id, async (id) =&gt; { user.value = await api.getUser(id) }, { immediate: true })\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- ✅ Fix 3: force a remount — simplest, but throws away the instance --&gt;\n&lt;RouterView :key="$route.fullPath" /&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Navigating — declaratively and programmatically:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- Declarative --&gt;\n  &lt;RouterLink to="/users/42"&gt;Profile&lt;/RouterLink&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- Named routes survive path refactors — prefer them --&gt;\n  &lt;RouterLink :to="{ name: 'user-detail', params: { id: user.id }, query: { tab: 'posts' } }"&gt;\n    {{ user.name }}\n  &lt;/RouterLink&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- Active styling: exact-active matches the full path --&gt;\n  &lt;RouterLink to="/users" active-class="on" exact-active-class="on-exact"&gt;Users&lt;/RouterLink&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- Full control over the rendered markup --&gt;\n  &lt;RouterLink v-slot="{ href, navigate, isActive }" to="/x" custom&gt;\n    &lt;li :class="{ active: isActive }"&gt;&lt;a :href="href" @click="navigate"&gt;X&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;/RouterLink&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">import { useRouter, useRoute } from 'vue-router'\n\nconst router = useRouter()   // for navigating\nconst route = useRoute()     // for reading — reactive\n\nrouter.push('/users/42')                                    // adds a history entry\nrouter.push({ name: 'user-detail', params: { id: 42 } })    // named\nrouter.replace('/login')                                    // no history entry\nrouter.back()                                               // history.back()\nrouter.go(-2)\n\n// push returns a promise resolving to a failure reason if a guard cancelled it\nconst failure = await router.push('/admin')\nif (failure) console.log('navigation aborted:', failure.type)\n\n// params are ALWAYS strings\nconst id = Number(route.params.id)\nconst page = Number(route.query.page ?? 1)\n\n// Updating query without losing the current route\nrouter.push({ query: { ...route.query, sort: 'name' } })</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Fetching in <code>onMounted</code> for a param-driven view.</strong> The instance is reused on param change, so it never refetches. Watch the param or use <code>props: true</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Blank page or 404 on hard refresh.</strong> <code>createWebHistory</code> needs a server rewrite to <code>index.html</code>. Netlify/Vercel do it with a config file; nginx needs <code>try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Comparing a param to a number.</strong> <code>route.params.id === 42</code> is always false — every param is a string.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Putting the catch-all route early.</strong> Matching is first-win, so it shadows every route below it and everything renders the 404.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code> for internal links.</strong> Triggers a full page reload and destroys the SPA. Use <code>&lt;RouterLink&gt;</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>app.use(router)</code> after <code>mount()</code>.</strong> Silently does nothing and every <code>&lt;RouterView&gt;</code> stays empty.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Modelling view state as route params.</strong> Sort order and pagination are query params; forcing them into the path makes routes rigid and order-dependent.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using <code>route</code> destructured.</strong> <code>const { params } = useRoute()</code> loses reactivity — the route object is reactive, its destructured pieces are not.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting <code>scrollBehavior</code>.</strong> Users navigate to a new page and land halfway down it.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Use named routes everywhere — paths change, names do not, and a typo in a name is catchable.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefer <code>props: true</code> (or the function form) so route components are plain input-driven components, testable without a router.</li>`,
        `<li>Model "which resource" as a path param and "how to view it" as query params, so views are bookmarkable and the back button restores UI state.</li>`,
        `<li>Lazy-import every route component — route-level splitting is the highest-value optimisation in most apps.</li>`,
        `<li>Configure <code>scrollBehavior</code> to restore saved positions and honour hash anchors.</li>`,
        `<li>Convert and validate params at the boundary — <code>Number()</code>, plus a regex constraint in the path where it helps.</li>`,
        `<li>Set up the SPA fallback on your host as part of the first deploy, not after the first bug report.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep the routes array declarative and push logic into guards and composables.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why doesn't my component refetch when the route param changes?"</strong><br>A: Because navigating from <code>/users/1</code> to <code>/users/2</code> matches the same route record, so Vue Router reuses the component instance rather than destroying and recreating it — <code>onMounted</code> doesn't run again. Fix it by watching <code>() =&gt; route.params.id</code> with <code>immediate: true</code>, or by using <code>props: true</code> and watching the prop, which also decouples the component from the router. As a blunt instrument you can put <code>:key="$route.fullPath"</code> on <code>&lt;RouterView&gt;</code> to force a remount, at the cost of throwing away the instance every time.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Route params vs query params?"</strong><br>A: Route params identify <em>which</em> resource and are part of the route's contract — <code>/orders/:id</code> won't match without one. Query params are optional, order-independent modifiers of the same view: filters, sort, pagination, UI state. The test: if removing it should 404, it's a route param; if removing it should just show defaults, it's a query param. Both are always strings.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "History mode vs hash mode?"</strong><br>A: <code>createWebHistory</code> uses the History API for clean URLs like <code>/users/42</code>, but the server must be configured to return <code>index.html</code> for unknown paths, because a hard refresh actually requests that path. <code>createWebHashHistory</code> puts the route after a <code>#</code>, which is never sent to the server, so it works on any static host with zero configuration — but the URLs are uglier and it's worse for SEO. There's also <code>createMemoryHistory</code> for SSR and tests.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "The app works when navigating but 404s on refresh. What's wrong?" (trap)</strong><br>A: The classic SPA deployment issue. In-app navigation never touches the server — the router swaps components client-side. A refresh sends the full path to the server, which has no route for <code>/users/42</code> and returns 404. The fix is a rewrite: <code>try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html</code> in nginx, a <code>_redirects</code> or <code>vercel.json</code> rule on static hosts, or switch to hash mode if you can't configure the server.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you navigate programmatically, and how is it different from <code>&lt;RouterLink&gt;</code>?"</strong><br>A: <code>useRouter()</code> gives the router instance: <code>push</code> adds a history entry, <code>replace</code> swaps the current one — right after a login so back doesn't return to the form — and <code>back</code>/<code>go</code> move through history. <code>&lt;RouterLink&gt;</code> renders a real anchor, so it's accessible, middle-clickable and crawlable — which is why it's the default for anything a user clicks. Programmatic navigation is for after an action: a successful save, a guard redirect.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>createRouter({ history, routes })</code>; <code>app.use(router)</code> <strong>before</strong> <code>mount()</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>createWebHistory</code> (clean URLs, needs server rewrite) · <code>createWebHashHistory</code> (zero config) · <code>createMemoryHistory</code> (SSR/tests).</li>`,
        `<li>Matching is <strong>top-down, first-win</strong> — catch-all <code>/:pathMatch(.*)*</code> goes last.</li>`,
        `<li><code>route.params</code> (path) vs <code>route.query</code> (optional modifiers). <strong>All values are strings.</strong></li>`,
        `<li>Same route + different param ⇒ <strong>instance reused</strong>, <code>onMounted</code> doesn't re-run.</li>`,
        `<li>Fix: <code>watch(() =&gt; route.params.id, …, { immediate: true })</code> · <code>props: true</code> · <code>:key="$route.fullPath"</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>useRoute()</code> = read (reactive, don't destructure) · <code>useRouter()</code> = navigate.</li>`,
        `<li><code>push</code> adds history · <code>replace</code> doesn't · both return a promise resolving to a failure if cancelled.</li>`,
        `<li>Param modifiers: <code>:id?</code> <code>:id+</code> <code>:id*</code> <code>:id(\\d+)</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <strong>named routes</strong>, lazy imports and <code>scrollBehavior</code>.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Nested Routes and Route Organisation',
      slug: 'nested-routes',
      order: 1,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Real applications are not flat. A dashboard has a persistent sidebar with swapping content; a settings page has tabs; an admin area has a shared layout wrapping a dozen sub-pages. Nested routes are how you express that hierarchy so the URL mirrors the UI, and it is the tool that stops you re-rendering the same chrome on every page.</p>`,
        `<p>Interviewers ask because the concept requires understanding that <code>&lt;RouterView&gt;</code> is <em>recursive</em> — a routed component can itself contain a <code>&lt;RouterView&gt;</code> for its children. Candidates who have only built flat apps usually miss that entirely.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>A route can have <code>children</code>. The parent's component renders in the top-level <code>&lt;RouterView&gt;</code>, and the child's component renders in a <code>&lt;RouterView&gt;</code> inside <em>that</em> component.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">{\n  path: '/users/:id',\n  component: UserLayout,           // renders in App.vue's &lt;RouterView&gt;\n  children: [\n    { path: '', component: UserOverview },        // /users/42\n    { path: 'posts', component: UserPosts },      // /users/42/posts\n    { path: 'settings', component: UserSettings },// /users/42/settings\n  ],\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The URL nesting mirrors the component nesting, which is the whole appeal: the shared chrome — header, tabs, sidebar — lives in <code>UserLayout</code> and is mounted once, while only the inner view swaps.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch7-nested-routes.svg|Diagram mapping a nested route config to a component tree, showing App's RouterView rendering UserLayout and UserLayout's own RouterView rendering the matched child view|RouterView is recursive: each nesting level renders into the RouterView of the level above it.}}`,
        `<p><strong>Path rules</strong> that trip people up: a child path <em>without</em> a leading slash is relative and appended to the parent; a child path <em>with</em> a leading slash is absolute and ignores the parent entirely. An empty child path (<code>''</code>) is the default child, rendered when the parent path matches exactly.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>Matching produces an array of <strong>matched route records</strong>, one per level — <code>route.matched</code>. Each level's component renders into the <code>&lt;RouterView&gt;</code> provided by the level above. When you navigate between siblings, only the deepest level unmounts and remounts; the parent layout is untouched, which is why nesting is also a performance decision, not just an organisational one.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Route meta</strong> travels with each record, and because <code>route.matched</code> is an array you can ask questions across the whole chain — <code>route.matched.some(r =&gt; r.meta.requiresAuth)</code> is the standard auth check, and it correctly protects children of a protected parent.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Named views</strong> handle the case where one level needs to render several components at once — a main area plus a sidebar. The route supplies a <code>components</code> object keyed by view name, and the template has multiple <code>&lt;RouterView name="…"&gt;</code>.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Layout strategies.</strong> Two approaches compete, and having an opinion is worth points:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Layout as a parent route</strong> — a pathless parent route (<code>path: ''</code> with a component and children) wrapping a group. Explicit and works with per-layout guards.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Layout chosen by meta</strong> — every route declares <code>meta.layout</code> and <code>App.vue</code> renders a dynamic <code>&lt;component :is&gt;</code>. Flatter route config, but the layout is resolved at render time rather than by the router.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p><strong>Organisation at scale.</strong> Once a routes array passes a few dozen entries, split it by feature into modules and compose them in <code>router/index.js</code>. That keeps route definitions next to the feature they belong to and makes deletion clean.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// router/index.js — nested routes with a shared layout\nconst routes = [\n  {\n    path: '/dashboard',\n    component: () =&gt; import('@/layouts/DashboardLayout.vue'),\n    meta: { requiresAuth: true },        // inherited by every child via route.matched\n    children: [\n      { path: '', name: 'dashboard', component: () =&gt; import('@/views/Overview.vue') },\n      { path: 'analytics', name: 'analytics', component: () =&gt; import('@/views/Analytics.vue') },\n      {\n        path: 'settings',\n        component: () =&gt; import('@/views/SettingsLayout.vue'),\n        children: [                       // three levels deep\n          { path: '', redirect: { name: 'settings-profile' } },\n          { path: 'profile', name: 'settings-profile', component: ProfileSettings },\n          { path: 'billing', name: 'settings-billing', component: BillingSettings,\n            meta: { requiresRole: 'admin' } },\n        ],\n      },\n    ],\n  },\n\n  // Absolute child path — ignores the parent prefix entirely\n  {\n    path: '/admin',\n    component: AdminLayout,\n    children: [\n      { path: 'users', component: AdminUsers },     // /admin/users\n      { path: '/logout', component: Logout },       // /logout  ← note the leading slash\n    ],\n  },\n]</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- App.vue — the top-level outlet --&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;RouterView /&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- layouts/DashboardLayout.vue — chrome + a nested outlet --&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;div class="dashboard"&gt;\n    &lt;AppSidebar /&gt;          &lt;!-- mounted once, survives child navigation --&gt;\n    &lt;AppHeader /&gt;\n    &lt;main&gt;\n      &lt;RouterView /&gt;        &lt;!-- children render HERE --&gt;\n    &lt;/main&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Named views — two components at one level:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">{\n  path: '/articles/:id',\n  components: {\n    default: ArticleBody,\n    sidebar: ArticleSidebar,\n    footer: ArticleFooter,\n  },\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;RouterView /&gt;                    &lt;!-- name=\"default\" implicitly --&gt;\n  &lt;RouterView name="sidebar" /&gt;\n  &lt;RouterView name="footer" /&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Breadcrumbs and auth from <code>route.matched</code> — the pattern that makes nesting pay off:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { computed } from 'vue'\nimport { useRoute } from 'vue-router'\n\nconst route = useRoute()\n\n// Every matched level, in order — parent → child\nconst breadcrumbs = computed(() =&gt;\n  route.matched\n    .filter(r =&gt; r.meta.title)\n    .map(r =&gt; ({ title: r.meta.title, to: r.path })),\n)\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;nav aria-label="Breadcrumb"&gt;\n    &lt;RouterLink v-for="(c, i) in breadcrumbs" :key="c.to" :to="c.to"&gt;\n      {{ c.title }}&lt;span v-if="i &lt; breadcrumbs.length - 1"&gt; / &lt;/span&gt;\n    &lt;/RouterLink&gt;\n  &lt;/nav&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// A guard that correctly protects children of a protected parent\nrouter.beforeEach((to) =&gt; {\n  if (to.matched.some(record =&gt; record.meta.requiresAuth) &amp;&amp; !auth.isLoggedIn) {\n    return { name: 'login', query: { redirect: to.fullPath } }\n  }\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Splitting routes by feature — how to keep this maintainable past fifty routes:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// router/routes/users.js\nexport default [\n  {\n    path: '/users',\n    component: () =&gt; import('@/layouts/UsersLayout.vue'),\n    children: [\n      { path: '', name: 'users', component: () =&gt; import('@/views/users/List.vue') },\n      { path: ':id', name: 'user-detail', props: true, component: () =&gt; import('@/views/users/Detail.vue') },\n    ],\n  },\n]\n\n// router/index.js\nimport users from './routes/users'\nimport orders from './routes/orders'\nimport admin from './routes/admin'\n\nconst routes = [\n  { path: '/', name: 'home', component: Home },\n  ...users,\n  ...orders,\n  ...admin,\n  { path: '/:pathMatch(.*)*', component: NotFound },   // still last\n]</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Leading slash on a child path.</strong> <code>path: '/posts'</code> inside a parent makes it absolute, so it resolves to <code>/posts</code>, not <code>/users/42/posts</code>. Almost always a typo.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting the <code>&lt;RouterView&gt;</code> in the parent component.</strong> The child matches but has nowhere to render, so the page looks empty with no error.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>No default child.</strong> Visiting the parent path renders the layout with an empty outlet. Add a <code>path: ''</code> child or a redirect.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Checking <code>route.meta</code> instead of <code>route.matched</code> in a guard.</strong> <code>route.meta</code> is merged from the chain in Vue Router 4, but <code>matched.some(...)</code> is the explicit, order-independent form and is what most codebases use.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Nesting too deeply.</strong> Past three levels the config becomes hard to follow and the URLs long; flatten with named routes instead.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting a parent layout to remount on child navigation.</strong> It does not — that is the feature. If it must react, watch the route inside it.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Named views with an unnamed outlet.</strong> A route using <code>components</code> without a <code>default</code> key renders nothing in the unnamed <code>&lt;RouterView&gt;</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Putting the catch-all inside a children array.</strong> It then only catches within that parent, which is occasionally what you want and usually not.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Nest when there is genuinely shared chrome; use flat routes with a layout otherwise.</li>`,
        `<li>Always give a parent route a default child (<code>path: ''</code>) or a redirect, so the bare parent URL is meaningful.</li>`,
        `<li>Put <code>meta</code> on the highest level it applies to and read it with <code>route.matched.some()</code>, so protection is inherited.</li>`,
        `<li>Split the routes array into per-feature modules once it grows past a screenful.</li>`,
        `<li>Name every route and navigate by name, so nesting changes do not break links.</li>`,
        `<li>Build breadcrumbs from <code>route.matched</code> rather than parsing the path.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep nesting to two or three levels; deeper hierarchies are usually a sign the UI needs rethinking.</li>`,
        `<li>Lazy-import both layouts and views so each feature is its own chunk.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do nested routes work?"</strong><br>A: A route can declare <code>children</code>. The parent's component renders into the <code>&lt;RouterView&gt;</code> one level up, and the matched child renders into a <code>&lt;RouterView&gt;</code> inside the parent's own template — so <code>&lt;RouterView&gt;</code> is effectively recursive. The URL hierarchy mirrors the component hierarchy, and the shared chrome in the parent is mounted once and survives navigation between siblings, so only the innermost view swaps.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What is <code>route.matched</code> and why does it matter?"</strong><br>A: The array of matched route records, one per nesting level, from the outermost inward. It's what makes hierarchical logic possible: <code>route.matched.some(r =&gt; r.meta.requiresAuth)</code> protects every descendant of a protected parent without repeating the flag, and mapping over it with a <code>meta.title</code> gives you breadcrumbs for free instead of parsing the URL.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why does my child route render at the wrong URL?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Almost certainly a leading slash on the child's path. Child paths without a leading slash are relative and get appended to the parent — <code>'posts'</code> under <code>/users/:id</code> becomes <code>/users/42/posts</code>. With a leading slash, <code>'/posts'</code> is absolute and resolves to <code>/posts</code>, ignoring the parent entirely. That's occasionally deliberate — a logout route inside an admin layout — but usually a typo.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How would you implement layouts?"</strong><br>A: Two options. Either a pathless parent route whose component is the layout, with the pages as children — explicit, and it lets you attach per-layout guards and meta. Or a <code>meta.layout</code> string on each route with <code>App.vue</code> rendering <code>&lt;component :is="layouts[route.meta.layout]"&gt;</code> — flatter config, easier to switch a single page's layout. I default to the parent-route approach because the router resolves it, which keeps the behaviour in one place.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What are named views?"</strong><br>A: A way to render several components at the same routing level. The route supplies a <code>components</code> object — <code>{ default: Body, sidebar: Sidebar }</code> — and the template has multiple outlets, <code>&lt;RouterView /&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;RouterView name="sidebar" /&gt;</code>. It's for the case where the sidebar's content is route-dependent but isn't a child of the main view. Note the unnamed outlet needs a <code>default</code> key or it renders nothing.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>children: []</code> on a route; the child renders into a <code>&lt;RouterView&gt;</code> <strong>inside the parent's template</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li>Child path without a leading slash = relative (appended). With one = <strong>absolute</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>path: ''</code> = default child, rendered when the parent matches exactly.</li>`,
        `<li>Parent layout is mounted once and survives sibling navigation.</li>`,
        `<li><code>route.matched</code> = array of records, outermost first → auth checks and breadcrumbs.</li>`,
        `<li><code>to.matched.some(r =&gt; r.meta.requiresAuth)</code> protects the whole subtree.</li>`,
        `<li>Named views: route <code>components: { default, sidebar }</code> + <code>&lt;RouterView name="sidebar" /&gt;</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Layouts: pathless parent route (preferred) or <code>meta.layout</code> + <code>&lt;component :is&gt;</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Split routes into per-feature modules; keep the catch-all last, at the top level.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Navigation Guards',
      slug: 'navigation-guards',
      order: 2,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Guards are where authentication, authorisation, unsaved-changes prompts, analytics and data prefetching live. They are also the part of Vue Router with the most ways to go wrong: an infinite redirect loop, a guard that runs before the auth store has hydrated, or a guard that silently blocks every navigation because you forgot to return anything.</p>`,
        `<p>Interviewers ask about guards because "how do you protect a route?" is a question with a lot of depth behind it — including the answer that route guards are <em>never</em> the security boundary.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>A <strong>navigation guard</strong> is a hook that runs during a navigation and can allow it, redirect it, or cancel it. There are three levels:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Global</strong> — <code>router.beforeEach</code>, <code>router.beforeResolve</code>, <code>router.afterEach</code>. Run for every navigation.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Per-route</strong> — <code>beforeEnter</code> on a route record. Runs only for that route.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>In-component</strong> — <code>onBeforeRouteUpdate</code> and <code>onBeforeRouteLeave</code> in setup, plus the Options-API <code>beforeRouteEnter</code>.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p>In Vue Router 4, a guard controls the navigation by its <strong>return value</strong>:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">router.beforeEach((to, from) =&gt; {\n  if (!auth.isLoggedIn) return { name: 'login' }   // redirect\n  if (blocked) return false                        // cancel\n  // return nothing / true                          → allow\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The old <code>next()</code> callback still works but is legacy; calling it more than once, or forgetting it entirely, is the source of the classic "navigation hangs forever" bug. The return-value form makes that impossible.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>Guards run in a fixed order, and knowing it answers most guard questions:</p>`,
        `<ol>`,
        `<li><code>beforeRouteLeave</code> on components being left</li>`,
        `<li><code>router.beforeEach</code> (global)</li>`,
        `<li><code>beforeRouteUpdate</code> on reused components</li>`,
        `<li><code>beforeEnter</code> on the route being entered</li>`,
        `<li>Async route components are resolved</li>`,
        `<li><code>beforeRouteEnter</code> on the entering components</li>`,
        `<li><code>router.beforeResolve</code> (global) — everything is confirmed, nothing has rendered</li>`,
        `<li><em>navigation confirmed</em></li>`,
        `<li><code>router.afterEach</code> (global) — cannot cancel anything</li>`,
        `<li>DOM updates, then component <code>onMounted</code> hooks</li>`,
        `</ol>`,
        `{{fig:ch7-guard-order.svg|Ordered flow of Vue Router navigation guards from beforeRouteLeave through beforeEach, beforeEnter, async component resolution, beforeResolve, navigation confirmation, afterEach and finally DOM update and mounted|The guard pipeline. Anything before confirmation can redirect or cancel; afterEach cannot.}}`,
        `<p><strong>Guards can be async.</strong> Returning a promise pauses the navigation until it settles — which is how you await an auth check or prefetch data before a page appears. It is also how you accidentally add a second of latency to every navigation, so keep them fast.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>The infinite-loop trap.</strong> A guard that redirects to <code>/login</code> without exempting <code>/login</code> itself redirects the redirect, forever. Vue Router detects some of these and throws, but the fix is always the same: check the target before redirecting.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>The initial-load trap.</strong> On a hard refresh, the guard may run before the auth store has restored a session from a token, so a logged-in user gets bounced to the login screen. The fix is to await a "session ready" promise inside the guard.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>onBeforeRouteLeave</code></strong> is the unsaved-changes guard — returning <code>false</code> cancels the navigation. Note it only covers in-app navigation; closing the tab or typing a new URL needs the native <code>beforeunload</code> event as well.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Guards are not security.</strong> They control which component renders in a browser the user fully controls. Anyone can call your API directly. Authorisation is enforced on the server, on every request; the guard is UX.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// router/guards.js — authentication and authorisation\nimport { useAuthStore } from '@/stores/auth'\n\nrouter.beforeEach(async (to, from) =&gt; {\n  const auth = useAuthStore()\n\n  // ⚠️ On a hard refresh the store may not have restored the session yet.\n  //    Without this, a logged-in user is bounced to /login on every refresh.\n  await auth.ready()\n\n  const requiresAuth = to.matched.some(r =&gt; r.meta.requiresAuth)\n\n  // ✅ Exempt the login route or this redirects forever\n  if (requiresAuth &amp;&amp; !auth.isLoggedIn &amp;&amp; to.name !== 'login') {\n    return { name: 'login', query: { redirect: to.fullPath } }   // remember where they wanted to go\n  }\n\n  // Already logged in? Don't show the login page.\n  if (to.name === 'login' &amp;&amp; auth.isLoggedIn) {\n    return { name: 'dashboard' }\n  }\n\n  // Role check\n  const required = to.meta.requiresRole\n  if (required &amp;&amp; !auth.hasRole(required)) {\n    return { name: 'forbidden' }\n  }\n\n  // return nothing → allow\n})\n\n// Titles and analytics belong in afterEach — it can't cancel anything\nrouter.afterEach((to, from, failure) =&gt; {\n  if (failure) return\n  document.title = to.meta.title ? \`\${to.meta.title} · Acme\` : 'Acme'\n  analytics.page(to.fullPath)\n})\n\n// Global error handling for lazy-loaded chunks that fail after a deploy\nrouter.onError((error) =&gt; {\n  if (error.message.includes('Failed to fetch dynamically imported module')) {\n    window.location.reload()   // new deploy invalidated the old chunk\n  }\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// Per-route guard — runs only for this route, and not on param changes\n{\n  path: '/admin/reports/:id',\n  component: ReportView,\n  beforeEnter: async (to) =&gt; {\n    const ok = await api.canViewReport(to.params.id)\n    if (!ok) return { name: 'forbidden' }\n  },\n}\n\n// An array of guards runs in order\n{ path: '/checkout', component: Checkout, beforeEnter: [hasItems, hasAddress] }</code></pre>`,
        `<p>In-component guards — unsaved changes and param updates:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref, onMounted, onUnmounted } from 'vue'\nimport { onBeforeRouteLeave, onBeforeRouteUpdate } from 'vue-router'\n\nconst isDirty = ref(false)\n\n// ① Block in-app navigation away from an unsaved form\nonBeforeRouteLeave((to, from) =&gt; {\n  if (!isDirty.value) return true\n  return window.confirm('You have unsaved changes. Leave anyway?')\n})\n\n// ② Native equivalent — covers tab close and manual URL entry,\n//    which the router guard cannot see\nfunction warn(e) { if (isDirty.value) e.preventDefault() }\nonMounted(() =&gt; window.addEventListener('beforeunload', warn))\nonUnmounted(() =&gt; window.removeEventListener('beforeunload', warn))\n\n// ③ Same route, different param — the component is reused, so this is\n//    where you refetch (an alternative to watching the param)\nonBeforeRouteUpdate(async (to, from) =&gt; {\n  if (to.params.id !== from.params.id) {\n    data.value = await api.get(to.params.id)\n  }\n})\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Redirect-after-login, the pattern that makes auth guards feel polished:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// The guard stored where the user was heading:\n//   { name: 'login', query: { redirect: to.fullPath } }\n\n// LoginView.vue\nconst route = useRoute()\nconst router = useRouter()\n\nasync function submit() {\n  await auth.login(credentials)\n  // replace, not push — the back button shouldn't return to the login form\n  router.replace(route.query.redirect || { name: 'dashboard' })\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Infinite redirect loop.</strong> Redirecting to <code>/login</code> without exempting <code>/login</code>. Always check the destination first.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Guard runs before auth state is restored.</strong> On a hard refresh the store is empty; await a session-ready promise or the user is logged out on every refresh.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting to return in a <code>next()</code>-style guard.</strong> The navigation hangs with no error. Use the return-value form.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Calling <code>next()</code> more than once.</strong> Undefined behaviour and a warning; another reason to prefer returns.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Slow async guards.</strong> Every navigation waits. Cache results and keep network calls out of the global guard where possible.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Treating guards as security.</strong> They gate rendering in a client the user controls. The server must authorise every request.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using <code>afterEach</code> to redirect.</strong> The navigation is already confirmed; it cannot cancel or change anything.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming <code>beforeEnter</code> fires on param change.</strong> It does not — the route record is the same. Use <code>onBeforeRouteUpdate</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Relying on <code>onBeforeRouteLeave</code> alone for unsaved changes.</strong> It cannot see a tab close; pair it with <code>beforeunload</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Calling <code>useAuthStore()</code> at module scope in the guards file.</strong> Pinia may not be installed yet; call it inside the guard.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Use the return-value form, never <code>next()</code>, in new code.</li>`,
        `<li>Drive guards from <code>meta</code> on route records and read them with <code>to.matched.some()</code>, so protection is declarative and inherited.</li>`,
        `<li>Await session restoration at the top of the global auth guard.</li>`,
        `<li>Always exempt the redirect target from its own guard.</li>`,
        `<li>Store the intended destination in a <code>redirect</code> query param and <code>replace</code> to it after login.</li>`,
        `<li>Put analytics, titles and non-blocking side effects in <code>afterEach</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep per-route concerns in <code>beforeEnter</code> rather than piling conditionals into the global guard.</li>`,
        `<li>Handle <code>router.onError</code> for failed dynamic imports — after a deploy, old chunk URLs 404 and a reload fixes it.</li>`,
        `<li>Enforce authorisation server-side and treat the guard as a UX affordance.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you protect a route?"</strong><br>A: Mark it with <code>meta.requiresAuth</code> and add a global <code>beforeEach</code> that checks <code>to.matched.some(r =&gt; r.meta.requiresAuth)</code> — using <code>matched</code> so children of a protected parent are covered — and returns a redirect to the login route if the user isn't authenticated, carrying <code>to.fullPath</code> in a query param so they land where they intended after logging in. Two details matter: exempt the login route itself or you loop forever, and await session restoration first, or a hard refresh logs everyone out. And the guard is UX, not security — the server authorises every request.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What's the guard execution order?"</strong><br>A: <code>beforeRouteLeave</code> on components being left, then the global <code>beforeEach</code>, then <code>beforeRouteUpdate</code> on reused components, then the route's <code>beforeEnter</code>, then async route components resolve, then <code>beforeRouteEnter</code>, then the global <code>beforeResolve</code>, then the navigation is confirmed, then <code>afterEach</code>, then the DOM updates and <code>onMounted</code> runs. Everything before confirmation can redirect or cancel; <code>afterEach</code> cannot, which is why it's the right place for titles and analytics.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you warn about unsaved changes?"</strong><br>A: <code>onBeforeRouteLeave</code> in the component — return <code>false</code>, or the result of a confirm dialog, to cancel the navigation. But that only covers in-app navigation; the router can't intercept a tab close or a manually typed URL, so pair it with a native <code>beforeunload</code> listener added on mount and removed on unmount. Both together is the complete answer.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Someone reports being logged out every time they refresh. What's happening?" (trap)</strong><br>A: The auth guard is running before the store has restored the session from its token — on a hard refresh the store starts empty, so <code>isLoggedIn</code> is false and the guard redirects. The fix is to expose a promise that resolves once session restoration has finished and <code>await</code> it at the top of the guard, so the check happens against real state rather than an uninitialised store.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "<code>beforeEnter</code> vs <code>beforeRouteUpdate</code>?"</strong><br>A: <code>beforeEnter</code> runs when you enter that route record from a <em>different</em> route — it doesn't fire when only a param changes, because the record is the same. <code>beforeRouteUpdate</code> fires exactly in that case, on the reused component, which makes it a natural place to refetch when the id changes. It's the guard equivalent of watching the param.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Levels: global (<code>beforeEach</code>/<code>beforeResolve</code>/<code>afterEach</code>) · per-route (<code>beforeEnter</code>) · in-component (<code>onBeforeRouteUpdate</code>/<code>onBeforeRouteLeave</code>).</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Return value controls it</strong>: nothing/<code>true</code> = allow · route location = redirect · <code>false</code> = cancel. <code>next()</code> is legacy.</li>`,
        `<li>Order: leave → beforeEach → update → beforeEnter → async components → beforeRouteEnter → beforeResolve → <strong>confirmed</strong> → afterEach → mounted.</li>`,
        `<li>Guards can be async — the navigation waits. Keep them fast.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Always exempt the redirect target</strong> or you loop.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Await session restoration</strong> before checking auth, or refresh logs users out.</li>`,
        `<li><code>to.matched.some(r =&gt; r.meta.requiresAuth)</code> covers nested children.</li>`,
        `<li><code>afterEach</code> can't cancel — use it for titles, analytics, scroll.</li>`,
        `<li>Unsaved changes = <code>onBeforeRouteLeave</code> <strong>plus</strong> <code>beforeunload</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Guards are <strong>UX, not security</strong>. Authorise on the server.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Lazy Loading and Route-Level Code Splitting',
      slug: 'lazy-loading-routes',
      order: 3,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>The single most effective performance change in most Vue applications is splitting the bundle by route. Without it, a user landing on the login page downloads the admin dashboard, the reporting charts and the rich-text editor before anything appears. With it, they download the login page.</p>`,
        `<p>Interviewers ask because it connects three areas — routing, bundling and performance — and because the follow-ups are practical: how do you group chunks, how do you prefetch, and what breaks after a deploy?</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>Instead of importing a route component at the top of the file, give the route a function that imports it:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ❌ Static — bundled into the main chunk, downloaded by everyone\nimport UserDetail from '@/views/UserDetail.vue'\n{ path: '/users/:id', component: UserDetail }\n\n// ✅ Dynamic — its own chunk, fetched when the route is first visited\n{ path: '/users/:id', component: () =&gt; import('@/views/UserDetail.vue') }</code></pre>`,
        `<p>That is the entire change. Vue Router already supports a function returning a promise, and the bundler recognises <code>import()</code> as a split point and emits a separate file. No configuration.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch7-code-splitting.svg|Comparison of a single large bundle downloaded on first paint versus a small entry chunk with per-route chunks fetched on demand, with a shared vendor chunk|Route-level splitting moves everything the first screen doesn't need out of the critical path.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><code>import()</code> returns a promise for the module. The bundler statically detects it, places that module and its unique dependencies in a separate chunk, and emits code to fetch the chunk on demand. Vue Router awaits the promise as part of the navigation — which is why the guard pipeline has an explicit "resolve async route components" step before the navigation is confirmed.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Chunk grouping.</strong> One chunk per route is not always optimal: fifty tiny chunks means fifty requests. Vite's <code>manualChunks</code> (and Webpack's magic comments) let you group related routes — all admin views in one chunk, all settings in another — which trades a slightly larger download for far fewer round trips.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Shared dependencies</strong> are hoisted automatically. If three lazy routes all import the same charting library, the bundler puts it in a shared chunk rather than duplicating it — but a library imported by both an eager and a lazy route ends up in the main bundle, which is worth checking when a split does not shrink the entry as much as expected.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Prefetching</strong> removes the latency cost. Modern bundlers can emit <code>&lt;link rel="modulepreload"&gt;</code> for likely-next chunks, and you can prefetch manually on hover — by the time the click lands, the chunk is cached. This is the trick that makes lazy routes feel as fast as eager ones.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>The stale-chunk problem</strong> is the production gotcha. Chunk filenames are content-hashed, so a deploy produces new names and deletes the old ones. A user with the page already open who navigates to a route they have not visited requests a chunk URL that no longer exists — the dynamic import rejects with "Failed to fetch dynamically imported module." The standard mitigation is <code>router.onError</code> plus a reload; the thorough one is keeping old assets on the CDN for a grace period.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// router/index.js — the standard shape\nconst routes = [\n  // Eager: on the critical path, don't split it\n  { path: '/', name: 'home', component: HomeView },\n\n  // Lazy: everything else\n  { path: '/dashboard', component: () =&gt; import('@/views/DashboardView.vue') },\n  { path: '/users/:id', component: () =&gt; import('@/views/UserDetail.vue'), props: true },\n\n  // A whole feature area, lazily loaded layout and children\n  {\n    path: '/admin',\n    component: () =&gt; import('@/layouts/AdminLayout.vue'),\n    children: [\n      { path: 'users', component: () =&gt; import('@/views/admin/Users.vue') },\n      { path: 'reports', component: () =&gt; import('@/views/admin/Reports.vue') },\n    ],\n  },\n]</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// vite.config.js — group related routes so you don't ship 50 tiny chunks\nexport default defineConfig({\n  build: {\n    rollupOptions: {\n      output: {\n        manualChunks(id) {\n          if (id.includes('/views/admin/')) return 'admin'\n          if (id.includes('/views/settings/')) return 'settings'\n          if (id.includes('node_modules/chart.js')) return 'charts'\n          if (id.includes('node_modules')) return 'vendor'\n        },\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Prefetching on hover — the cheapest perceived-performance win available:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\n// The import map lets us trigger the same dynamic import the router will use.\n// Calling it twice is free — module resolution is cached.\nconst prefetchers = {\n  dashboard: () =&gt; import('@/views/DashboardView.vue'),\n  reports: () =&gt; import('@/views/admin/Reports.vue'),\n}\n\nfunction prefetch(name) {\n  prefetchers[name]?.()\n}\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- By the time the click lands, the chunk is already in the cache --&gt;\n  &lt;RouterLink to="/dashboard" @mouseenter="prefetch('dashboard')"&gt;Dashboard&lt;/RouterLink&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Handling the two production realities — loading feedback and stale chunks:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ① A global progress bar, driven by the guard pipeline\nimport NProgress from 'nprogress'\n\nrouter.beforeEach(() =&gt; { NProgress.start() })\nrouter.afterEach(() =&gt; { NProgress.done() })\nrouter.onError(() =&gt; { NProgress.done() })\n\n// ② Stale chunks after a deploy: the hashed filename no longer exists\nrouter.onError((error, to) =&gt; {\n  const isStaleChunk =\n    /Failed to fetch dynamically imported module|Importing a module script failed/.test(error.message)\n\n  if (isStaleChunk) {\n    // Full reload picks up the new index.html and its new chunk names.\n    // Guard against a reload loop with a one-shot flag.\n    if (!sessionStorage.getItem('chunk-reload')) {\n      sessionStorage.setItem('chunk-reload', '1')\n      window.location.assign(to.fullPath)\n    }\n  }\n})\n\nrouter.afterEach(() =&gt; sessionStorage.removeItem('chunk-reload'))</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// Component-level splitting inside a route, for the heavy parts\nimport { defineAsyncComponent } from 'vue'\n\n// Only downloaded if the user actually opens the editor\nconst RichTextEditor = defineAsyncComponent({\n  loader: () =&gt; import('@/components/RichTextEditor.vue'),\n  loadingComponent: Skeleton,\n  delay: 200,\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Splitting the landing route.</strong> You have added a network round trip to first paint. Keep the initial route eager.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>No loading feedback.</strong> On a slow connection the click appears to do nothing. A progress bar or a <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> fallback is not optional.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Ignoring stale chunks after deploys.</strong> Users with the tab open hit a hard failure. Handle <code>router.onError</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Over-splitting.</strong> Fifty routes, fifty chunks, fifty requests. Group by feature.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming a split shrank the entry bundle.</strong> A dependency also imported by an eager route stays in the main chunk. Verify with a bundle visualiser.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>A dynamic import with a fully variable path.</strong> <code>import(userInput)</code> cannot be statically analysed, so the bundler either fails or includes everything matching; keep at least a static prefix.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting that guards run before the chunk resolves.</strong> A slow chunk on a guarded route means the guard has already passed and the user is staring at nothing.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Lazy-loading tiny components.</strong> The request overhead exceeds the bytes saved.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Lazy-load every route except the one that renders first.</li>`,
        `<li>Group routes into feature chunks with <code>manualChunks</code> rather than shipping one chunk per file.</li>`,
        `<li>Show navigation progress — a top progress bar is the conventional, cheap solution.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefetch on hover or focus for primary navigation; it makes lazy routes feel instant.</li>`,
        `<li>Handle <code>router.onError</code> for failed dynamic imports and reload once, with a loop guard.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep old chunk files on the CDN for a grace period after each deploy so open tabs keep working.</li>`,
        `<li>Measure with <code>rollup-plugin-visualizer</code> before and after — split what is actually large.</li>`,
        `<li>Split heavy components inside a route as well: editors, charts, maps, PDF viewers.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you lazy-load routes and why?"</strong><br>A: Give the route a function returning a dynamic import — <code>component: () =&gt; import('@/views/X.vue')</code> — instead of a static import. The bundler treats <code>import()</code> as a split point and emits a separate chunk fetched on first visit, and Vue Router awaits it as part of the navigation. The win is that the initial bundle only contains what the first screen needs; without it, someone landing on the login page downloads the entire admin area first.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Can you over-split?"</strong><br>A: Yes. One chunk per route means one request per navigation, and on a high-latency connection many small requests are slower than a few larger ones — plus each chunk carries its own overhead and HTTP round trip. I group related routes into feature chunks with Vite's <code>manualChunks</code>: all admin views together, all settings together, vendor separately. And I never split the landing route, since that just adds a round trip to first paint.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Users get 'Failed to fetch dynamically imported module' after a deploy. What's going on?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Chunk filenames are content-hashed. A deploy generates new hashes and removes the old files, but a user with the app already open is still running the previous <code>index.html</code>, which references the old chunk names. Navigating to a route they hadn't visited requests a file that no longer exists. Mitigate with <code>router.onError</code> — detect that message and do a full reload so the new HTML and chunk names are picked up, with a session flag to prevent a reload loop. The more robust fix is retaining old assets on the CDN for a grace period.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you make lazy routes feel fast?"</strong><br>A: Prefetch. Trigger the same dynamic import on <code>mouseenter</code> or focus of the link, so the chunk is in the module cache by the time the click lands — calling the import twice costs nothing. Modern bundlers can also emit <code>modulepreload</code> hints for likely-next chunks. Beyond that, show a progress bar during navigation so a slow load never looks like a dead click.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Where else would you code-split besides routes?"</strong><br>A: Heavy components that are conditionally rendered — rich text editors, charting libraries, maps, PDF viewers, date pickers — with <code>defineAsyncComponent</code>. Those are often larger than the route that hosts them. I'd also check whether a big dependency can be imported more narrowly, since a library pulled in by one eager route ends up in the main bundle no matter how much route splitting you do.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>component: () =&gt; import('@/views/X.vue')</code> — that's the whole change.</li>`,
        `<li>Bundler sees <code>import()</code> → separate chunk; router awaits it during navigation.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Don't split the first route</strong> — you'd add a round trip to first paint.</li>`,
        `<li>Group with Vite <code>manualChunks</code>; 50 routes ≠ 50 chunks.</li>`,
        `<li>Shared deps hoist automatically; a dep also used eagerly stays in the main bundle.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefetch on hover/focus — the import is cached, so the click is instant.</li>`,
        `<li>Show progress: <code>beforeEach</code> start / <code>afterEach</code> done.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Stale chunks after deploy</strong> → <code>router.onError</code> + one guarded reload; keep old assets on the CDN.</li>`,
        `<li>Also split heavy components with <code>defineAsyncComponent</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Measure with <code>rollup-plugin-visualizer</code>.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
  ],
}
